<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pelagonia</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pelagonia is a plain and historical region in the south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-macedonia">North Macedonia</a>, around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bitola">Bitola</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prilep">Prilep</a>, extending into north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>. In antiquity it was the land of the Pelagones, and in the Roman settlement of 167 BCE it formed one of the four republics into which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-macedon">Macedon</a> was divided. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> it was a Byzantine and Bulgarian borderland and the site of a battle in 1259.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pelagonia.jpg" alt="Pelagonia" width="320" height="133" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Petar Milošević · CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pelagonija.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
